WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.940
How can losing nearly half your enzyme make purification better?

2
00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:09.340
Follow the gold target through one sample, and the answer becomes visible.

3
00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:12.520
Gold tokens mark active target.

4
00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:16.860
The whole gold-and-grey pile represents total protein.

5
00:00:20.417 --> 00:00:24.377
Before any separation, you need an assay that finds the target.

6
00:00:25.657 --> 00:00:29.077
For an enzyme, its reaction reports activity in units.

7
00:00:30.417 --> 00:00:33.657
Total protein measures every protein in the same tube.

8
00:00:34.717 --> 00:00:41.217
Our crude homogenate has 100,000 activity units and 10,000 milligrams of protein.

9
00:00:42.657 --> 00:00:44.537
Divide activity by total protein.

10
00:00:45.957 --> 00:00:48.597
Specific activity is 10 units per milligram.

11
00:00:49.617 --> 00:00:55.777
The salt cut keeps 90% % of target activity but only 30% % of total protein.

12
00:00:57.017 --> 00:00:59.757
Specific activity triples, from 10 to 30.

13
00:01:01.137 --> 00:01:03.377
Purity rose although some target was lost.

14
00:01:04.450 --> 00:01:06.050
Here is the guiding idea.

15
00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:12.010
A good step keeps a larger fraction of target activity than total protein.

16
00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:17.530
Specific activity rises by the ratio of those retained fractions.

17
00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:23.900
Now the late affinity step keeps about 55% % of the activity it receives.

18
00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:27.300
It keeps only 2% % of the protein.

19
00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:30.800
Is losing nearly half the activity a failure?

20
00:01:36.483 --> 00:01:42.243
No. 55% % divided by 2% % is about 27.

21
00:01:43.663 --> 00:01:48.903
Specific activity jumps from 1,100 to 30,000 units per milligram.

22
00:01:50.183 --> 00:01:52.023
This is the best step of the whole route.

23
00:01:53.017 --> 00:01:55.457
Two other numbers describe the trade-off.

24
00:01:56.877 --> 00:02:00.157
Yield is activity now divided by starting activity.

25
00:02:01.757 --> 00:02:06.297
Fold-purification is specific activity now divided by its starting value.

26
00:02:07.557 --> 00:02:10.277
One tracks quantity; the other tracks enrichment.

27
00:02:11.417 --> 00:02:15.897
Across this route, activity yield falls to 36%.

28
00:02:16.417 --> 00:02:19.337
Yet specific activity rises 3,000-fold.

29
00:02:20.617 --> 00:02:24.637
Step by step, both the enrichment factors and the losses multiply.

30
00:02:25.817 --> 00:02:28.857
The ledger judges a step; it does not choose one.

31
00:02:30.177 --> 00:02:34.917
Choose a property where the target differs from contaminants: solubility, charge,

32
00:02:35.717 --> 00:02:36.957
size, or selective binding.

33
00:02:38.257 --> 00:02:39.757
Then assay every fraction.

34
00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:45.720
In its simplest form, the ledger treats activity per milligram of target as

35
00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:49.420
constant. Inactivation can hide enrichment.

36
00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:52.720
Removing an inhibitor can mimic it.

37
00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:55.760
The text below handles these cases.

38
00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:00.220
So purification is not a contest to lose nothing.

39
00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:06.820
Ask whether each step keeps a larger fraction of target activity than total protein.

40
00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:10.280
That is how selective loss creates purity.
